# context boundary

**Read [`memory/context-boundary.md`](../memory/context-boundary.md) before going further.** This rule exists to
fire on the topic and send you there; the knowledge itself lives in one place, not two.

**Why it is worth the read:** The thinness is the feature and it looks like an omission, so the default instinct -- helpfully re-inlining what was deliberately moved out -- undoes the work without anyone deciding to.

## Before you touch anything

- The rule is: keep what has no cue, move what has a cue. Retrieval fails when the trigger is an absence, so a constraint nothing prompts you to look up stays always-on.
- Always-on text is paid for on every turn whether relevant or not. Adding a paragraph here is a recurring cost, not a one-off.
- Defensive one-liner ("beware", "do not") goes to Gotchas. Explanatory ("here is how", "here is why") goes to a topical.

---

*This rule is Cursor's retrieval mechanism for `memory/`. On Claude Code the same
topical is reached through the Read-when cue in `memory.md`. Cursor does not act on
that pointer, so without this rule the topicals were unreachable here -- the rule is
the selector, the topical is the content, and neither duplicates the other.*
